  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 22,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.751,
            0.063,
            0.834,
            0.08
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fractions"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.121,
            0.785,
            0.142
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Expressing a fraction in lowest terms can also be done in steps."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.143,
            0.833,
            0.212
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Suppose we want to express "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 3 6 } { 6 0 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " in lowest terms. First, we notice that both the numerator and denominator are even. So, we divide both by 2, and see that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{36}{60}   =   \\frac{18}{30}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.449,
            0.97,
            0.55,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.218,
            0.832,
            0.265
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Both the numerator and denominator are even again, so we can divide them each by 2 again; we get "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 8 } { 3 0 }   =   \\frac { 9 } { 1 5 }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.275,
            0.833,
            0.322
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We now notice that 9 and 15 are both multiples of 3, so we divide both by 3 to get "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 9 } { 1 5 }   =   \\frac { 3 } { 5 }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.325,
            0.834,
            0.379
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now, 3 and 5 have no common factor other than 1, so, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 3 6 } { 6 0 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " in lowest terms is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 3 } { 5 } ."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.386,
            0.834,
            0.466
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Alternatively, we could have noticed that in "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 3 6 } { 6 0 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , both the numerator and denominator are multiples of 12 : we see that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "36 = 3 \\times 12"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "6 0 = 5 \\times 1 2"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Therefore, we could have concluded that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{36}{60} \\equiv \\frac{3}{5}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " straight away."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.47,
            0.833,
            0.515
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Either method works and will give the same answer! But sometimes it can be easier to go in steps."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.527,
            0.301,
            0.55
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Figure it Out"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.556,
            0.624,
            0.613
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Express the following fractions in lowest terms: a. "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 7 } { 5 1 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " b. "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{\\left ( 64 \\right ) }{144}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " e. "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{126}{147}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " d. "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 5 2 5 } { 1 1 2 }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.638,
            0.478,
            0.664
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7.7 Comparing Fractions"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.676,
            0.834,
            0.766
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Which is greater, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{4}{5}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " or "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 7 } { 9 } ?"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " It can be difficult to compare two such fractions directly. However, we know how to find fractions equivalent to two fractions with the same denominator. Let us see how we can use it:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.12,
            0.771,
            0.259,
            0.803
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_22_equation_13.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac{4}{5} = \\frac{4 \\times 9}{5 \\times 9} = \\frac{36}{45}"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.12,
            0.813,
            0.266,
            0.846
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_22_equation_14.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac { 7 } { 9 } \\quad = \\quad \\frac { 7   \\times   5 } { 9   \\times   5 } \\quad = \\quad \\frac { 3 5 } { 4 5 } ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.422,
            0.82,
            0.627,
            0.9
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "45 is a common multiple of 5 and 9, so we can use 45 as a common denominator."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.659,
            0.82,
            0.803,
            0.897
          ],
